78515625, 0.0031795501708984375, 0.02935791015625, -0.0009274482727050781, 0.0009217262268066406, 0.044769287109375, 0.0097198486328125, 0.01012420654296875, 0.01058197021484375, 0.0323486328125, -0.01230621337890625, -0.0025730133056640625, -0.0240478515625, -0.000278472900390625, -0.007213592529296875, -0.01340484619140625, 0.0291595458984375, -0.00405120849609375, -0.004077911376953125, -0.0017652511596679688, 0.004077911376953125, -6.67572021484375e-06, 0.0074310302734375, 0.01406097412109375, 0.0242767333984375, 0.003108978271484375, -0.0200042724609375, -0.00899505615234375, -0.00347137451171875, -0.006641387939453125, 0.006397247314453125, 0.012054443359375, -0.0056915283203125, 0.0278778076171875, -0.03271484375, -0.006999969482421875, -0.0185699462890625, -0.01346588134765625, 0.025848388671875, -0.00759124755859375, 0.0165252685546875, -0.040740966796875, 0.003406524658203125, -0.018829345703125, -4.863739013671875e-05, -0.00417327880859375, 0.0070343017578125, 0.0017633438110351562, -0.00836944580078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**\n1. Introduction\n**\n\nWe present an extension of the permanental process model\u2014a probabilistic framework for describing random configurations of points\u2014by incorporating explicit fixed effects (covariates). Permanental processes are random point process models that encode attractive dependency structures among collections of points, enabling the capture of higher-order, nontrivial interactions absent from Poisson or Cox processes. However, classical permanental process models generally lack direct mechanisms for incorporating covariate information (e.g., experimental conditions, systematic trends), which limits interpretability and flexibility. By contrast, generalized additive models (GAMs) or Cox processes allow explicit covariate effects but cannot capture the higher-order dependencies intrinsic to permanental processes. Our contribution is to augment the permanental process with fixed effects, enabling joint modeling of structured dependence and interpretable covariate effects.\n\n**Notation Summary:**  \n| Symbol | Description                                       |\n|--------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| $x$    | Location in domain $\\mathcal{X}$ (e.g., time, space) |\n| $z$    | Covariate/features at $x$ ($z \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$)     |\n| $f$    | Nonparametric effect, function on $\\mathcal{X}$       |\n| $k$    | RKHS kernel function for $f$                         |\n| $k^*$  | Modified kernel ensuring orthogonality to $z$         |\n| $Z$    | $n \\times p$ covariate matrix over data               |\n| $K$    | $n \\times n$ kernel Gram matrix $k(x_i, x_j)$         |\n\n**\n2. Model Specification\n**\n\n**2.1 Model Formulation**\n\nLet $\\mathcal{X}$ denote the observation domain (e.g., time interval or spatial region). For $n$ observed points $S = \\{x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\} \\subset \\mathcal{X}$, each $x_i$ is associated with a covariate vector $z_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$, forming the covariate matrix $Z = [z_1^\\top;\\ldots;z_n^\\top]$. The (log-)intensity function at $(x, z)$ is defined as:\n\\[\n\\lambda(x, z) = z^\\top \\beta + f(x)\n\\]\nwhere $\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$ are fixed effect coefficients, and $f:\\mathcal{X} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is a smooth, data-adaptive function in the RKHS associated with kernel $k$. Unlike Poisson or Cox processes, $\\lambda(x,z)$ parametrizes the likelihood via the permanental process law. The probability of observing the configuration $S$, with associated covariates $\\{z_i\\}$, is:\n\\[\nP(S \\mid f, \\beta) = \\frac{1}{Z_{f, \\beta}} \\mathrm{per}\\left([k^*(x_i, x_j)]_{i, j=1}^n\\right) \\prod_{i=1}^n \\exp\\big(\\lambda(x_i, z_i)\\big)\n\\]\nwhere $\\mathrm{per}(\\cdot)$ is the matrix permanent, $k^*$ is the modified kernel (see below), and $Z_{f, \\beta}$ is the normalization constant integrating the process over all admissible point configurations on $\\mathcal{X}$.\n\n**2.2 Identifiability and Modified Kernel Construction**\n\nNaively, $f$ and $\\beta$ are non-identifiable if $f$ can mimic effects from $z^\\top\\beta$. To ensure identifiability, we restrict $f$ to the orthocomplement of the span of $z \\mapsto z^\\top \\gamma$ (for all $\\gamma$) in the observed data. This is achieved by modifying the kernel $k$ to $k^*$ so that, for all $x$ and $x'$,\n\\[\nk^*(x, x') = k(x, x') - h(x)^\\top (Z^\\top Z)^{-1} h(x'), \\quad \\text{where } h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n z_i k(x, x_i).\n\\]\nThis functional definition generalizes the Gram matrix projection: for the observed data, the resulting Gram matrix $K^*$ aligns with $(I-P_Z)K(I-P_Z)$, where $P_Z = Z(Z^\\top Z)^{-1} Z^\\top$ projects onto the column space of $Z$.\n\n**2.3 Prior Choice**\n\nWe place independent zero-mean Gaussian process prior on $f$, $f \\sim \\mathcal{GP}(0, k)$, facilitating flexible, smooth domain effects, with regularization controlled by the kernel $k$. The fixed effect coefficients $\\beta$ are assigned a Gaussian prior $\\beta \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\tau^2 I_p)$. Setting $\\tau^2$ large recovers unpenalized regression, but regularization is recommended\u2014smaller $\\tau^2$ can mitigate overfitting, especially under collinearity or high-dimensional $z$.\n\n**\n3. Methodology\n**\n\n**3.1 Generative Model and Likelihood**\n\nThe covariate-augmented permanental process assumes:  \n1. Observed points $S = \\{x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\} \\subset \\mathcal{X}$ are generated according to the permanental process with parameterized log-intensity $\\lambda(x, z)$.  \n2. Covariates $z_i$ are either observed features or fixed design variables.\n3. The full joint probability for configuration $S$ (with $\\{z_i\\}$) is as above, with normalization $Z_{f,\\beta}$ integrating over all finite subsets of $\\mathcal{X}$ (see Macchi 1975 for details).\n\n**3.2 Model Fitting and Algorithm**\n\nWe jointly estimate $(\\beta, f^*)$ by optimizing the penalized log-likelihood:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}(\\beta, f^*) = -\\log P(S \\mid f^*, \\beta) + \\frac{1}{2} \\|f^*\\|_{k^*}^2 + \\frac{1}{2\\tau^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2.\n\\]\nThe representer theorem (see Sch\u00f6lkopf et al., 2001) guarantees that the solution $f^*$ admits an expansion:\n\\[\nf^*(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k^*(x, x_i), \\text{ with } Z^\\top f^* = 0.\n\\]\nThe optimization typically proceeds via quasi-Newton (e.g., L-BFGS) methods, initialized at $(\\beta^{(0)}, \\alpha^{(0)})$, with convergence checked via function or parameter tolerance ($\\|\\theta^{(t)} - \\theta^{(t-1)}\\| < \\epsilon$) or maximum iterations.\n\n**Pseudocode Outline:**\n1. Compute $K=[k(x_i, x_j)]$, $Z$, $P_Z=Z(Z^\\top Z)^{-1}Z^\\top$, and $K^*=(I-P_Z)K(I-P_Z)$.\n2. Maximize penalized log-likelihood for $(\\beta,\\alpha)$, for instance with L-BFGS.\n3. Recover $f^*(x) = \\sum_i \\alpha_i k^*(x, x_i)$ and $\\lambda(x,z)$.\n\n**Worked Example:**  \nConsider $n=3$ events at $x_1=0.1$, $x_2=0.5$, $x_3=0.9$ with binary indicators $z_1=0$, $z_2=1$, $z_3=1$. Let the kernel be $k(x,x') = \\exp(-|x - x'|^2/0.1)$. Then $Z = [0;1;1]$, $K$ is computed for these $x_i$. Calculate $P_Z$, then $K^*$. The fitted $f^*$ will have $Z^\\top f^* = 0$, i.e., its average over each group is zero. The estimated $\\hat{\\beta}$ captures the adjusted group mean effect. (See Supp. Figure 1 for schematic.)\n\n**3.3 Comparison to Related Models**\n\nStandard GAMs and Cox processes model covariate effects but lack the permanental process' ability to encode higher-order attraction/repulsion via the permanent. Determinantal point processes (DPPs) model repulsion (negative dependence) and enable efficient computation, but cannot model clustering/attraction. Our model provides:\n- explicit interpretability via $\\beta$;\n- ability to capture attractive (\u201cclumping\u201d) dependencies absent in DPPs and Poisson models;\nbut requires strong assumptions on the dependence form; model misspecification (e.g., if dependencies are not as in permanental processes) may yield biased inference.\n\n**3.4 Practical Considerations and Regularization**\n\n- **Covariate Design:** $Z$ should be full column rank; otherwise, $\\beta$ is not identifiable.\n- **Measurement Error:** Biases in $z$ (Carroll et al., 2006) affect $\\beta$ (see Supp. B for bias correction simulation).\n- **Regularization:** For small $\\tau^2$ (more regularization), shrinkage increases and spurious fixed effects are suppressed (see Supp. Fig. 2 for illustration of varying $\\tau^2$ on $\\hat{\\beta}$). Cross"}}